# Denylist, not an allowlist: a newly added directory the Gradle build needs is included by default,
# so this file can only enlarge the build context, never starve `./gradlew bootJar` of an input it reads.
#
# Rationale: .planning/quick/260811-nh1-fix-broken-api-docs-swagger-endpoint-exc/260811-nh1-PLAN.md. Do NOT
# exclude anything the build reads: gradlew, gradle/ (including gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar),
# build.gradle, settings.gradle, src/.

# Version control and agent/planning state -- never read by the build, and .claude/.planning/.gsd
# routinely hold local worktree state (including live Gradle lock files) that once caused an
# outright `docker build` failure when a concurrent process held one.
.git
.gradle
.claude
.planning
.gsd
.dev

# Build output -- regenerated fresh inside the build stage; shipping the host's stale copy in
# would be wasted context transfer at best and stale-artifact confusion at worst.
build

# Editor/IDE state -- never read by the build.
.idea
.vscode

# Repo tooling not read by the Gradle build itself.
.githooks
.github
.gitattributes

# Documentation -- large, frequently-changing, and never consulted by `./gradlew bootJar`.
docs
*.md
CLAUDE-SECURITY-*

# Credentials: `.env*` carries DB_USER/DB_PASS.
#
# Absent this line the Dockerfile's `COPY . .` sweeps it into the build-stage layer. The runtime stage
# copies only the built jar, so .env never reaches the final image either way, but it would still sit in an
# intermediate/cached build-stage layer. build.gradle's git-hooks bootstrap block is guarded by a
# rootProject.file('.git').exists() check, so excluding .git above makes it skip cleanly rather than fail.
# This does NOT affect `docker compose up`: Compose reads .env from the HOST for variable substitution,
# never from the build context.
.env*
